    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Done. The build passes with all tests green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe spec required all **400 / 409 / 429** rejection responses to return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`. Nine escalation handlers were still returning a plain `application/json` `Map.of(\"errors\", \u2026)` body with no `status` member. I converted each to the project's established `ProblemDetail` pattern (already used by `ValidationExceptionHandler`, `GeneralExceptionHandler`, etc.):\n\n- **400**: `MissingOwnerFields`, `DisposableEmailDomain`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidTelephone`, `PostcodeOutOfRange`\n- **409**: `CityAtCapacity`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n- **429**: `DailyOwnerLimitExceeded`\n\nEach now builds a `ProblemDetail` via the shared `ProblemDetails.build(ex, status, detail)` helper (which sets `type=about:blank`, `title`=exception simple name, `status`, `detail`, plus `timestamp`) and returns it as `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>`. Spring's return-value handler serializes `ProblemDetail` as `application/problem+json` automatically, so the content type and `status` member now match. I preserved each handler's existing `errors` array (e.g. `[\"city\"]`, `ex.getFields()`) as an extra problem-detail property, keeping the documented field-name contract intact. HTTP status codes are unchanged.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `mvnw compile` \u2014 clean.\n- `AcceptanceTest.coreRejectionIsProblemJson` (the new test asserting `application/problem+json` + `$.status == 400`) \u2014 passes.\n- Full `mvnw test` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, 1 test, 0 failures.\n\nI confirmed no function classes emit 400/409/429 directly \u2014 all such rejections flow through these escalation handlers, so the coverage is complete.",
