        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change matches the codebase's existing idiom exactly. The shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (already in the package, already used by `ValidationExceptionHandler` and `DataIntegrityViolationExceptionHandler`) centralizes the RFC7807 common fields (type, title, status, detail, timestamp, schemaValidationErrors placeholder), and each converted handler only expresses what's genuinely specific to it: the status, a human-readable `detail`, and its own `errors` field. That is exactly the right split of responsibility.\n\n- No duplication is introduced \u2014 the boilerplate lives once in the helper, and the per-handler bodies stay small.\n- Each handler remains single-purpose; none becomes a catch-all.\n- The `ProblemDetails` helper is not becoming a dumping ground \u2014 it still takes only `(ex, status, detail)` and the handlers layer their own properties on top, consistent with how `ValidationExceptionHandler` adds its richer `errors`/`schemaValidationErrors`.\n- The repeated status (passed to `build` and again to `ResponseEntity.status`) is the pre-existing pattern in the surrounding handlers, so it reads as natural here rather than as a new wart.\n- Javadoc was updated on each handler to describe the new problem+json shape, keeping docs coherent with behavior.\n\nA maintainer of this codebase would find this a natural, additive extension of the existing escalation-handler convention."
